  >
    <h2>Context</h2>
    <p>
      Transferring files between a PC and an iPhone without a cable, without cloud and without
      Internet is a common problem. Existing solutions like <strong>AirDrop</strong> are limited to
      the Apple ecosystem, and <strong>LocalSend</strong> requires installing an app on both
      devices.
    </p>
    <p>
      The goal of <strong>LocalShare</strong> was to offer an alternative:
      <strong>zero installation on mobile</strong>, everything goes through the Safari browser. The
      PC hosts a lightweight Node.js server, and the iPhone accesses a web page over the local
      network.
    </p>
    <p>Three requirements:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Offline</strong> — work on a router without Internet, or the iPhone personal
        hotspot
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Video streaming</strong> — play a video directly in Safari, without waiting for the
        full download
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Real-time chat</strong> — exchange messages instantly between the two devices
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Architecture</h2>
    <p>The system relies on a single Node.js server that does everything:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Express 5</strong> serves the static web page and exposes the REST API (upload,
        download, list, delete)
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>ws</strong> (WebSocket) handles real-time chat on the same port as HTTP
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>better-sqlite3</strong> persists file metadata and message history (WAL mode for
        performance)
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Multer</strong> handles multipart uploads with a 2 GB limit
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>qrcode-terminal</strong> displays an ASCII QR code in the terminal on startup
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      On the iPhone side, everything is <strong>vanilla JavaScript</strong>: native WebSocket for
      chat, Fetch for file list, XMLHttpRequest for upload with progress bar, and a native HTML5
      video player that leverages Range 206 support.
    </p>

    <h2>Development steps</h2>

    <h3>1. Initialization and database</h3>
    <p>
      Node.js project creation, dependency installation. SQLite initialized with two tables:
      <code>files</code> (file metadata) and <code>messages</code> (chat history). WAL mode enabled
      for better concurrent read/write performance.
    </p>

    <h3>2. File API with Range 206 support</h3>
    <p>
      This is the most important technical point. The <code>GET /api/download/:id</code> route reads
      the <code>Range</code> header from the request, computes the requested portion, and returns
      the <code>206 Partial Content</code> status with <code>Content-Range</code>,
      <code>Accept-Ranges: bytes</code> and <code>Content-Length</code> headers. Without this
      mechanism, Safari would have to download the entire video before playing it.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      This is the same mechanism that allows YouTube or Netflix to stream videos without
      downloading them entirely.
    </blockquote>

    <h3>3. WebSocket for chat</h3>
    <p>
      Integration of <code>ws</code> on the same HTTP server via <code>http.createServer(app)</code>.
      On connection, the server sends the history of the last 100 messages. On message receipt, it
      validates the content, inserts it into SQLite, then broadcasts it to all connected clients.
      On the client side, automatic reconnection (2 seconds) handles Wi-Fi drops.
    </p>

    <h3>4. Vanilla frontend</h3>
    <p>
      Two-panel interface (chat + files), responsive (CSS Grid), dark mode with CSS variables.
      Upload uses <code>XMLHttpRequest</code> to display a real progress bar (via
      <code>upload.onprogress</code>). The emoji picker inserts characters directly into the input
      field.
    </p>

    <h3>5. QR code and IP detection</h3>
    <p>
      On startup, the server detects the first non-internal IPv4 address with
      <code>os.networkInterfaces()</code>, builds the URL <code>http://&lt;IP&gt;:3000</code>, and
      displays an ASCII QR code with <code>qrcode-terminal</code>. The iPhone just scans it with the
      camera app to open the page.
    </p>

    <h3>6. Finishing touches and error handling</h3>
    <p>
      Global error middleware that intercepts Multer errors (413 for oversized files, 400 for form
      errors) and generic errors (500). Client-side size validation (2 GB). System messages in chat
      for connections/disconnections. File delete button with confirmation.
    </p>

    <h2>Challenges encountered</h2>

    <h3>🌐 Range 206 support</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: without Range, Safari downloads the entire video before displaying
      it. For a 500 MB video, that's several minutes of waiting.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution</strong>: manual parsing of the <code>Range</code> header, portion
      computation, 206 response with proper headers. Safari can now play the video immediately and
      seek in the timeline.
    </p>

    <h3>📱 Safari iOS restrictions</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: Safari blocks camera/microphone access over plain HTTP (requires
      HTTPS or localhost). This prevents considering a WebRTC video call in the browser.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution</strong>: the project focuses on file sharing and chat, which don't require
      getUserMedia. Video calling is documented as a future improvement requiring a native app or
      Capacitor.
    </p>

    <h3>🔥 Firewall and AP isolation</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: some routers (especially guest networks) enable client isolation,
      preventing the iPhone from seeing the PC. The Windows firewall can also block port 3000.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution</strong>: documented in the README. Disable AP isolation in the router
      interface, set the network as "private" on Windows, allow port 3000.
    </p>

    <h3>🎥 Demo recording on Wayland</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: ffrec and wf-recorder don't work on GNOME/Wayland (black video or
      <code>wlr-screencopy-unstable-v1</code> error).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution</strong>: use <strong>Kooha</strong>, a screen recorder based on PipeWire +
      xdg-desktop-portal, compatible with Wayland.
    </p>

    <h2>Result</h2>
    <p>LocalShare is <strong>functional</strong>:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>✅ Node.js server listening on the local network and displaying a QR code</li>
      <li>✅ iPhone connects via Safari by scanning the QR code (zero installation)</li>
      <li>✅ Real-time chat via WebSocket, history persisted in SQLite</li>
      <li>✅ File upload with progress bar (2 GB limit)</li>
      <li>✅ Download with Range 206 support: native video streaming in Safari</li>
      <li>✅ File deletion with confirmation</li>
      <li>✅ Responsive interface with dark mode and emoji picker</li>
      <li>✅ Error handling (Multer, 413, 500) and automatic WebSocket reconnection</li>
      <li>✅ Works on router without Internet and on iPhone hotspot</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What I learned</h2>
    <p>This project taught me <strong>three things</strong>:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>The HTTP protocol is richer than it seems</strong> — Range 206 support is invisible
        in daily use, but it's what makes video streaming possible. Understanding HTTP headers in
        depth changes how you design an API.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>WebSocket is ideal for local real-time</strong> — no polling, no latency, no server
        overload. A persistent connection is enough for smooth chat between two devices.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The browser is a powerful platform</strong> — with vanilla JavaScript, you can
        build a chat, an upload with progress, a video player with seek, and a responsive interface.
        No framework needed for a project of this size.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Future improvements</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>PIN access code</strong> — currently, anyone on the same Wi-Fi can access the page.
        A PIN displayed in the terminal would restrict access.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>End-to-end encryption</strong> — files travel over plain HTTP on the LAN.
        Client-side encryption (AES-GCM via Web Crypto API) would strengthen security.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Thumbnails for images and videos</strong> — generate a thumbnail client-side with
        canvas to make the list more pleasant.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Integrated video player</strong> — instead of opening a new tab, a modal with
        <code>&lt;video controls&gt;</code> that reads the Range stream directly.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Multiple chat rooms</strong> — currently a global two-person chat. Per-session rooms
        would allow multiple simultaneous conversations.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Video demonstration</h2>
    <p>Here is a short demonstration of the project in action:</p>
    <div class="my-8 rounded-xl overflow-hidden border" style="border-color: var(--border-subtle);">
      <video 
        controls 
        class="w-full" 
        preload="metadata"
      >
        <source src={img('video-demo')} type="video/webm" />
        Your browser does not support video playback.
      </video>
    </div>
  </ProjectLayout>
</BaseLayout>